# Third-party notices

`hypr-shortcut-launcher` itself is MIT licensed; see [LICENSE](LICENSE). This
file records the one thing that licence does not settle on its own. It is kept
separate from `LICENSE` on purpose: GitHub detects a project's licence by
matching the licence text in `LICENSE`, and a file that continues past the end
of the MIT text is reported as "Other" instead of MIT.

## GTK 4 — LGPL-2.1

The binary links GTK 4 dynamically against whatever GTK 4 the user's system
provides. It does not vendor, statically link, or patch GTK 4.

The LGPL requires that anyone receiving a copy of the binary may replace the
GTK 4 shared libraries it uses with modified versions, and that the
corresponding GTK 4 sources be available to them. Dynamic linking against the
system library satisfies the first of those provided the second holds, which on
most distributions it does — the GTK 4 sources ship in the development package:

| Distribution | GTK 4 source package |
| --- | --- |
| Arch | `gtk4` (the `-dbg`/`gtk4-devel` variants where split) |
| Fedora | `gtk4-devel` |
| Debian, Ubuntu | `libgtk-4-dev` |
| openSUSE | `gtk4-devel` |

`gtk4-layer-shell`, the same LGPL-2.1 licensing, is linked the same way.

Building from this repository produces the same binary, with both libraries
again supplied by the build host's package manager, so a distro package that
builds from source and depends on its own `gtk4` and `gtk4-layer-shell` is
carrying a binary that is straightforwardly LGPL-compliant.

What would *not* be compliant, and is not done here: statically linking GTK 4
or gtk4-layer-shell into the binary, patching either library, or shipping a
build with the relinking ability removed.
